{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3646","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3646","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3646\",\"parent_revision\":\"975faaecf99865c17673d52838518b2579dbe303\",\"root_revision\":\"bb46f2aaa3c1a9e004b7562f1360353c4965eeda98c93d233b4215e21fc4dcf4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-19\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen a session's work becomes work on an existing backlog item, the orchestrator must enter that item's workflow through `/dh:work-backlog-item #N`. That skill loads the item's grooming, research and plan. The orchestrator must also:\\n\\n- load `/agent-orchestration:delegate` before it dispatches a sub-agent;\\n- audit a sub-agent's report before it relays the report's claims;\\n- read the governing architecture and the linked issues before it proposes new backlog items;\\n- run `/dh:verify-done` before it reports work as complete;\\n- load `receiving-pr-reviews` after each push.\\n\\n`AGENTS.md` (\\\"Skill, Command, and Agent Usage Policy\\\") states these steps as a policy table. The table takes effect only when the orchestrator checks it.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA partial fix reached a PR that claimed to close a P1 issue. It relied on a premise that #3059 had already disproved.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nWork on an existing backlog item goes through that item's workflow, even when a session's scope grows into it partway through. A skipped gate is visible without a human finding it.\\n\\n**Hypothesis**: the orchestrator checks the policy table only at stage changes it recognizes. A session whose scope grows partway through is never re-classified, so no trigger fires.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nWork on an existing backlog item goes through that item's workflow, even when a session's scope grows into it partway through. A skipped gate is visible without a human finding it.\\n\\n**Hypothesis**: the orchestrator checks the policy table only at stage changes it recognizes. A session whose scope grows partway through is never re-classified, so no trigger fires.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation 2026-09-19: the repo owner's review of PR #3642\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-19\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Observed\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe session began as worktree cleanup. It grew into shipping a fix for #3060, a P1 item that was already groomed and had plan `Pe6bcc5c2`. The orchestrator:\\n\\n- dispatched a raw sub-agent that worked from the architecture spec file alone. The agent's input carried no item grooming: not #3060's RT-ICA reconciliation, and not #3059's evidence that MAP has no size limit;\\n- opened PR #3642 with \\\"Closes #3060\\\". The change meets only part of #3060's acceptance criteria;\\n- offered new backlog items for findings that #3057 and #3059 already track, before it read `docs/component-architecture.md` and `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`;\\n- reported the work as done without `/dh:verify-done`;\\n- wrote \\\"closes #3060\\\" into commit `7c9203b05`. This repo squash-merges with `COMMIT_MESSAGES`, so that merge closes #3060 whatever the PR body says.\\n\\nThe repo owner found each skipped step. The orchestrator did not.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"12b9a965cde093b9c3dfd31a28d610a3c472a7e5d94dffe12118a80c3ae9be5c\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVoH2YQ\"}"}